ecture (2015–2023).\n\n# Zhang and Zhang's unimodality conjecture for Clar covering polynomials\n\nAt least 10 years old · documented by 2015\n\nLet $H$H be a hexagonal system, meaning a 2-connected finite plane graph whose interior faces are regular hexagons of side length one. Let $R(H)$R(H) be the resonance graph of $H$H, and let $C(R(H),x)$C(R(H),x) be its cube polynomial; equivalently, let $\\zeta(H,x)$ζ(H,x) denote the Clar covering polynomial of $H$H.\n\nZhang and Zhang's conjecture. The polynomial\n\n$$C(R(H),x)=\\zeta(H,x)$$C(R(H),x)=ζ(H,x)\n\nis unimodal.\n\nThis conjecture concerns resonance graphs of hexagonal systems, a subclass of median graphs. The corresponding conjecture for all median graphs is false, but this restricted conjecture remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nYan-Ting Xie, Yong-De Feng and Shou-Jun Xu, “A relation between the cube polynomials of partial cubes and the clique polynomials of their crossing graphs”, arXiv:2303.14671 (2024).\n\n## Additional references\n\n3 papers in this index state this conjecture (2015–2023). The statement above is taken from the most recent of them; the others are arXiv:2004.12822, arXiv:1508.05024.\n\ngraph-polynomialshexagonal-systemsunimodality\n\n0 0\n\nPosted 2 months ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://lacim.uqam.ca/en/seminar.html)\nciteturn9search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week;   * 2026-10-02 ... In 2024, Hikita announced a proof of the Stanley-Stembridge Conjecture on e-positivity of certain chromatic symmetric functions, which had been open for 32 years. ...   * 2025-11-21 ... Motivated by the Stanley-Stembridge conjecture that such chromatic functions are e-positive, we show that the allowable coloring weights for such a graph are the lattice points of a permutahedron, and give a formula for the dominant coloring weight.\n  * 2026-10-02\n\nNicolas Avila, LACIM\nLuck and Magic for Pitman-Stanley Polytopes and Parking Functions\n\nAbstract\n\nPitman and Stanley introduced a rich family of lattice polytopes now commonly called Pitman–Stanley polytopes. These polytopes appear in different contexts, including probability, statistics, and algebraic combinatorics, and it is well known that there is a relationship between these polytopes and parking functions by counting lattice points. In this talk, we will show that the relationship between these two families is deeper, particularly within the framework of Ehrhart theory. Parking functions refine the Ehrhart polynomial in the “magic basis” using a “lucky cars” statistic, this implies a positivity phenomenon in that basis and implies the $h^\\ast$-polynomial is real-rooted. And the $h^\\ast$-polynomial can also be characterized by parking functions by an ascent statistic.\n\n  * 2026-09-25\n\nAlex Vidinas, Cornell University\nA dimer view on Fox's trapezoidal conjecture\n\nAbstract\n\nAbstract: Fox’s conjecture (1962) states that the sequence of absolute values of the coefficients of the Alexander polynomial of alternating links is trapezoidal. \nAbstract\n\nThe study of proper colorings of graphs is a longstanding and important area in mathematics. The Four Color theorem for planar graphs is one famous success, and this simple problem in cartography launched a vast theory leading to many concrete applications, as well as to the algebraic study of chromatic polynomials and symmetric functions, which also arise in the geometry of Hessenberg varieties.\n\nIn 2024, Hikita announced a proof of the Stanley-Stembridge Conjecture on e-positivity of certain chromatic symmetric functions, which had been open for 32 years. This breakthrough leaves open, and gives rise to, many followup mysteries. We will discuss the significance of the symmetric functions arising in graph colorings, and give an overview of some of the important open problems in the area. This discussion will include joint work with Joseph Pappe and Kyle Salois, towards the question, “When is the chromatic quasisymmetric function symmetric?”.\n\n(Part of the CRM-ISM Colloquium Series – note the special time and location).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiscrete Mathematics and Theoretical Computer Science (https://dmtcs.episciences.org/7411/pdf)\nciteturn9search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.5 years ago; Flip-sort and combinatorial aspects of ... Flip-sort is a natural sorting procedure which raises fascinating combinatorial questions. ... arXiv:2003.04912v3  [math.CO]  26 Apr 2021\nDiscrete Mathematics and Theoretical Computer Science\nDMTCS vol. 22:2, 2021, #4\nFlip-sort and combinatorial aspects of\npop-stack sorting\nAndrei Asinowski1\nCyril Banderier2\nBenjamin Hackl1\n1 University of Klagenfurt, Klagenfurt, Austria\n2 University of Sorbonne Paris Nord, Villetaneuse, France\nreceived 12thMar. 2020, revised 7th Apr. 2021, accepted 7thApr. 2021\nFlip-sort is a natural sorting procedure which raises fascinating combinatorial questions. It ﬁnds its\nroots in the seminal work of Knuth on stack-based sorting algorithms and leads to many links with\npermutation patterns. We present several structural, enumerative, and algorithmic results on permutations\nthat need few (resp. many) iterations of this procedure to be sorted. In particular, we give the shape of the\npermutations after one iteration, and characterize several families of permutations related to the best and\nworst cases of ﬂip-sort. En passant, we also give some links between pop-stack sorting, automata, and\nlattice paths, and introduce several tactics of bijective proofs which have their own interest.\nUUU We dedicate this article to Don Knuth, for his 1111000th birthday. . . up to a permutation! UUU\nKeywords: permutation, stack sorting, generating function, ﬁnite automaton, generating tree, lattice path\n1\nIntroduction and deﬁnitions\n2\n1.1\nFlip-sort and pop-stack sorting . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n2\n1.2\nSummary of our results\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n3\n2\nResults concerning one iteration of the ﬂip-sort algorithm\n6\n2.1\nStructural characterization of pop-stacked permutations . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n6\n2.2\nPop-stacked permutations with a ﬁxed number of runs . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n7\n2.3\nA functional equation for pop-stacked permutations and the corresponding\npolynomial-time algorithm for the enumeration . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n13\n2.4\nAsymptotics of pop-stacked permutations\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n18\n3\nBest cases of ﬂip-sort: permutations with low cost\n19\n3.1\nNotation and earlier work . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n19\n3.2\nThe pre-images of 1-pop-stack-sortable permutations . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n19\n3.3\n2-pop-stack-sortable permutations and lattice paths . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n20\narXiv:2003.04912v3  [math.CO]  26 Apr 2021\n4\nWorst-cases of ﬂip-sort: permutations with high cost\n25\n4.1\nDeﬁnitions and notation . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n25\n4.2\nThe worst case: n −1 iterations, and a key statistic: bandwidth of permutations\n27\n4.3\nThe penultimate step of ﬂip-sort: the theorem about Im(Tn−2). . . . . . . . . . .\n32\n4.4\nSkew-layered permutations: a family of worst cases . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n36\n5\nConclusion\n37\nISSN 1365–8050\n© 2021 by the author(s)\nDistributed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiscrete Mathematics and Theoretical Computer Science (https://lipn.fr/~banderier/Papers/dmtcs2020.pdf)\nciteturn9search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Flip-sort and combinatorial aspects of pop-stack sorting ... useful for the next natural step: the average-case analysis of ﬂip-sort. ... This research was supported by the Austrian Science Fund (FWF) in the framework of the\nFlip-sort and combinatorial aspects of pop-stack sorting\n37\n5\nConclusion\nIn this article, we have seen that a simple sorting procedure like ﬂip-sort has many interesting\ncombinatorial and algorithmic facets. Our analysis of the best cases and the worst cases of this\nalgorithm, our results on the underlying poset, the links with lattice paths, are just some ﬁrst\nsteps towards a more reﬁned understanding of this process.\nEn passant, we listed a few conjectures, which, we hope, could tease the desire of the reader\nto work on this topic. The optimal cost of the computation of pop-stacked permutations is still\nopen; other challenges are a ﬁner analysis of the automata which recognize them (when the\nnumber of runs is ﬁxed), the existence of closed-form formulas, a method to solve puzzling\nfunctional equations like the one given in Theorem 10.\nWe also believe that several of the notions and structures introduced in this article can also be\nuseful for the next natural step: the average-case analysis of ﬂip-sort. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRainbow induced-path conjecture for K_r-free graphs | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/345059/rainbow-induced-path-conjecture-for-k-r-free-graphs)\nciteturn9search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; The source notes partial progress and that the assertion without the rainbow condition is known; the rainbow statement remains open. ... Kang and Viresh Patel, “Structure and colour in triangle-free graphs”, arXiv:1912.13328 (2020).\n\n# Rainbow induced-path conjecture for $K_r$K r​-free graphs\n\n7 years old · traced to 2019\n\nLet $r\\geq 3$r≥3, and let $G$G be a properly coloured $K_r$K r​-free graph with chromatic number $\\chi$χ, where $K_r$K r​ denotes the complete graph on $r$r vertices. A rainbow induced path is an induced path whose vertices all receive distinct colours.\n\nRainbow induced-path conjecture. For each $r\\geq 3$r≥3, every properly coloured $K_r$K r​-free graph of chromatic number $\\chi$χ contains a rainbow induced path of length $\\chi^{1/(r-2)}$χ 1/(r−2).\n\nThis generalises the triangle-free case and is related to quantitative bounds for independent sets in $K_r$K r​-free graphs. The source notes partial progress and that the assertion without the rainbow condition is known; the rainbow statement remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n  1. N. R. Aravind, Stijn Cambie, Wouter Cames van Batenburg, Rémi de Joannis de Verclos, Ross J. Kang and Viresh Patel, “Structure and colour in triangle-free graphs”, arXiv:1912.13328 (2020).\n\nchromatic-numberinduced-pathsrainbow-subgraphs--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPapers from 2026-10-29 - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/date/2026-10-29)\nciteturn9search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: yesterday; The Cranston--Li--Su--Wang--Xu (2026) conjecture is true ... Together with the Larman--Rogers--Seidel theorem, these results give maximum cardinality $d(d+1)/2$ for $9\\le d\\le21$ and $275$ for $d=22$, without exhaustive enumeration. ... In this paper, we give combinatorial interpretations of (i) the Graham-positive coefficients in the expansion of double Schur polynomials in the double fundamental quasisymmetric basis and (ii) the coefficients in the expansion of Grothendieck polynomials in the grove basis. ... 2026-10-29 v5 ... We show that every graph $G$ with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges admits a balanced partition $V_1,\\ldots,V_k$ such that \\begin{equation*} \\max_{\\substack{A\\in\\binom{[k]}{\\ell}}}e_G\\left(\\bigcup_{i\\in A}V_i\\right)\\le\\frac{\\ell^2}{k^2}m+\\frac{\\ell^2(k-\\ell)}{k^2}(n-1)+\\frac{\\ell(k-\\ell)}{k(k-1)}\\sqrt{\\left(\\binom{k}{\\ell}-1\\right)m}. \\end{equation*} In the case $\\ell=2$, our result confirms a conjecture of Bollobás and Scott in a stronger form. ... Over $\\C$ we obtain the sharp Alon--Boppana lower bound for the second eigenvalue of the evolution operator, leading to the definition of \\emph{Ramanujan evolution algebras} as optimal expanders.\nThe telescoping functions also turn out to be shared among some Foatic actions, implying that mixing certain actions still yields a constant average on closed walks.\n\n2026-10-29\n\nDave Goel\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nThe Cranston--Li--Su--Wang--Xu (2026) conjecture is true\n\nLet $A$ be a finite abelian group. A nowhere-zero $A$-flow of a graph gives every edge a direction and a non-zero value in $A$, so that at every vertex the sum of the values on the entering edges equals the sum on the leaving edges. Such a flow can be changed along a cycle: going around the cycle, add a fixed non-zero element of $A$ to the value of every edge that points forward and subtract it from the value of every edge that points backward. The Cranston--Li--Su--Wang--Xu (2026) conjecture states that if $A$ has at least four elements, then in every graph in which all degrees are even, any two nowhere-zero $A$-flows are joined by a chain of such changes through nowhere-zero $A$-flows. We prove that all zeros of $H_d(t)$ are real and negative for $3\\le d\\le1000$ and for every $d\\ge2^{62}$; for $d\\le2$ the polynomial is constant. Expanding $H_d(t)$ in refined Eulerian polynomials does not suffice, since a coordinate is already negative at $d=7$. Let $A_{d,i}(t)$ enumerate all permutations of $\\{1,\\ldots,d\\}$ with first entry $i$ by number of descents. For $c\\ge0$, we prove that every nonnegative linear combination of $A_{d,1},\\ldots,A_{d,d}$ and the adjacent differences $A_{d,r+1}-cA_{d,r}$, $2\\le r\\le d-2$, is real-rooted for all $d\\ge4$ simultaneously if and only if $c\\le2-\\sqrt3$. This reduces real-rootedness to the positivity of explicit weighted tails of the coordinates. An exact certificate checks these inequalities for $d\\le1000$, and coefficient estimates based on Darroch's theorem on the mode of a sum of Bernoulli variables prove them for $d\\ge2^{62}$; the range $1001\\le d<2^{62}$ remains open. Along the way we identify $P_d$, up to lattice equivalence, with twice a chain-poset permutahedron and with the Newton polytope of a discriminant, which transfers the known cube structure and volume of these models to $P_d$. We also show that the coefficient of $t^{d-1}$ in $H_d(t)$ counts strong tournament score sequences for $d\\ge3$, confirming a conjecture of Rehberg in this range.\n\nIn 2005, a polygon $S_n\\subseteqΛ_n$ was constructed, and it was conjectured that $Λ_n=S_n$; this holds for $n\\le3$. We show that the conjecture fails for every $n\\ge5$. The vertices of $S_n$ are eigenvalues of circulant Laplacians built from powers of the directed $n$-cycle. To first order, no perturbation of these circulants moves such an eigenvalue out of $S_n$; however, adding chords with nonconstant weights moves it along an edge of $S_n$ to first order and, with an explicit coefficient, beyond the supporting line of this edge to second order. Consequently, every edge of $S_n$ not incident to $0$ or $1$ contains interior points of $Λ_n$, except possibly, for even $n$, the two horizontal edges, whereas the four edges incident to $0$ or $1$ lie on $\\partialΛ_n$. For $n=4$, whose only other edges are horizontal, the question was left open in the previous versions; it has since been settled, $Λ_4=S_4$, by a computer-assisted proof of L. Marshall, after an earlier claim by D.Z. Liu, who also proved that $Λ_n$ is not convex for $n\\ge5$. We certify an explicit weighted counterexample for $n=5$, lying only $1.5\\cdot10^{-3}$ outside $S_5$, and an unweighted one for $n=7$: the directed $6$-cycle with a duplicated vertex. Its eigenvalue outside $S_7$ is also an eigenvalue of the two-chord digraph that ends the above perturbation; for the analogous digraphs of order $n$, the excess is asymptotic to $\\frac{π^2}{2n^4}$. We also observe that $S_n$ is a zonotope, describe $Λ_n$ in terms of eigenvectors, and prove that $S_m\\subseteq S_n$ whenever $m\\mid n$.\n\n2026-10-29 v2\n\nZhanping Yang\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nBounds for Unions of Several Parts in Balanced Graph Partitions\n\nLet $k\\ge3$ and $1\\le \\ell\\le k-1$. We study balanced $k$-partitions of a graph for which the union of any $\\ell$ parts induces few edges. We show that every graph $G$ with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges admits a balanced partition $V_1,\\ldots,V_k$ such that \\begin{equation*} \\max_{\\substack{A\\in\\binom{[k]}{\\ell}}}e_G\\left(\\bigcup_{i\\in A}V_i\\right)\\le\\frac{\\ell^2}{k^2}m+\\frac{\\ell^2(k-\\ell)}{k^2}(n-1)+\\frac{\\ell(k-\\ell)}{k(k-1)}\\sqrt{\\left(\\binom{k}{\\ell}-1\\right)m}. \\end{equation*} In the case $\\ell=2$, our result confirms a conjecture of Bollobás and Scott in a stronger form.\n\n2026-10-29 v2\n\nGhislain Fourier\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nThe Hibi-Li Face-Number Conjecture\n\nWe prove the face-number conjecture of Hibi and Li: for every finite poset, the order polytope has no more faces of any given dimension than the chain polytope. More generally, the face numbers increase weakly along the admissible family of chain--order polytopes when one order coordinate is replaced by a chain coordinate. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\narXiv Troller (https://arxiv-troller.com/?q=paper%3A+2609.22344)\nciteturn9search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Submitted: Oct 2026 | Updated: Oct 2026\n\nDahlberg, She, and van Willigenburg conjectured that every $e$-positive tree has maximum degree at most three. We prove this conjecture for caterpillars.\nThis answers a question of Bukh for all such primes and, in particular, settles the case $L={1,4,6}\\subseteq\\mathbb{F}_{19}$ singled out on his problem list. When $0\\notin L$, a standard polynomial argument shows that the exponent $1/3$ is optimal. The proof combines a geometric construction in characteristic two with an incidence matrix factorization over $\\mathbb{F}_p$. The same construction yields integer lifts $L'$ of the prescribed sets $L$ and symmetric $L'$-matrices of rational rank $O_{p,L}(n^{1/3})$. When $0\\notin L'$, this improves Bukh's general quadratic lower bound, giving matrices of order $Ω_{p,L}(r^3)$ and rational rank at most $r$.\n\n$e$-Positive caterpillars have maximum degree at most three\n\nDavion Q. B. Tang, David G. L. Wang, Monica M. Y. Wang\n\narXiv:2610.39481\n\nAuthors: Davion Q. B. Tang, David G. L. Wang, Monica M. Y. Wang\n\nSubmitted: Oct 2026 | Updated: Oct 2026\n\nDahlberg, She, and van Willigenburg conjectured that every $e$-positive tree has maximum degree at most three. We prove this conjecture for caterpillars. Our proof introduces a specialization--cone method: we evaluate chromatic symmetric functions under finite-support specializations that are nonnegative on the elementary symmetric functions, express the evaluations through transfer matrices, and certify negativity using finitely generated cones. Two specializations and three cones handle all caterpillars remaining after a connected-partition reduction, with no bound on the number or spacing of their trivalent vertices. The required cone inclusions and strict sign inequalities are certified by exact rational arithmetic. We also exhibit two infinite families of non-spider caterpillars of maximum degree four that admit connected partitions of every type, showing that Wolfgang's criterion alone leaves cases requiring further obstructions. Our result also reduces Li's criterion on $e$-positivity for caterpillars of maximum degree three to an explicit condition on the positions of trivalent vertices.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRainbow-independent set (https://kiwix.drnathanhutchins.com/content/wikipedia_en_all_maxi_2026-02/Rainbow-independent_set)\nciteturn9search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; ; Patel, Viresh (2020-03-15). \"Structure and colour in triangle-free graphs\". arXiv:1912.13328 [math.CO]. ... \"Rainbow independent sets on dense graph classes\". arXiv:2001.10566 [math.CO].\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStolen Thoughts (https://stolen-thoughts.com/)\nciteturn9search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: yesterday; AIME 2025 ... Problem In $\\triangle ABC$ points $D$ and $E$ lie on $\\overline{AB}$ so that $AD < AE < AB$, while points $F$ and $G$ lie on $\\overline{AC}$ so that $AF < AG < AC$. ... If $A_1A_2+A_1A_{11}$ can be expressed as $\\frac{m\\sqrt{n}-p}{q}$ for positive integers $m,n,p,q$ with $n$ squarefree and $\\gcd(m,p,q)=1$, find $m+n+p+q$. ... A_11. x_2 = x_4 = x_6 = x_8 = x_10 = a. x_3 = x_5 = x_7 = x_9 = x_11 = b.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDatabase | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/database?tag=unimodality)\nciteturn9search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; The polynomials $\\SYT(\\lambda)^{\\maj}(q)$\\SYT(λ)\\maj(q) are nearly unimodal but not unimodal for partitions $\\lambda$λ or $\\lambda'$λ′ in the following cases: any parti… ... open ehrhart-theory lattice-polytopes unimodality\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2025-03-01&page=14&range=31)\nciteturn9search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday;   * Breaking Barriers: Combinatorial Algorithms for Non-monotone Submodular Maximization with Sublinear Adaptivity and $1/e$ Approximation ... @misc{2502.07062, author = {Yixin Chen and Wenjing Chen and Alan Kuhnle}, title = {{B}reaking {B}arriers: {C}omb